; FIX-2. NIGHT EDGE -- GREEN. Program decision D10.
;
; Assigned by the geometry packets to the EDGE meshes/rims they author (the postpile's summit cap).
; A normal matte surface by day; an emissive rim once `bt_darkness` rises. It EMITS and never
; LIGHTS -- see `night_edge.gdshader` header for why that distinction is the whole design, and
; for why the rim is a geometric band rather than a glowing face.
;
; TWO OVERRIDES ONLY: the body colour and the edge colour. Everything about HOW the edge
; behaves -- band width, the darkness onset, the Fresnel safety net -- lives in
; the shader, so all four night-edge materials behave identically and a tuning pass is one
; file rather than four that drift apart.
;
; WHY THIS FILE DID NOT EXIST UNTIL NOW. BT-9 authored blue, cyan and red and stopped; BT-2's bake
; driver left the postpile cap in the group "night_edge" with a comment saying a follow-up packet
; would re-point it "mechanically". That packet was never written, so green's aid has never lit and
; `night_edge_red.tres` sat referenced by nothing. FIX-2 is that follow-up. (2026-08-28.)
[gd_resource type="ShaderMaterial" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Shader" path="res://resources/shaders/night_edge.gdshader" id="1_edge"]

[resource]
render_priority = 0
shader = ExtResource("1_edge")
; The DAYLIGHT body: the shared mid gray from the section palette (neutral_gray.tres colour).
; The rims are ordinary neutral geometry in daylight -- Talon rule "most geometry stays neutral
; gray/white" applies to them too, and D10 aids are a NIGHT feature, not permanent decoration.
; Byte-identical to the other three, deliberately: the four must read as one family by day.
shader_parameter/albedo = Color(0.227, 0.227, 0.23, 1)
; The NIGHT edge: green's section hue, DERIVED rather than eyeballed, and the derivation is the
; only defensible way to add a fourth member to a set authored before BT-9b moved the palette.
;
; The three shipped night edges are all HSV(<section hue>, 0.86, 0.850) -- blue 225 deg, cyan 182,
; red 8 -- i.e. one saturation and one value, hue as the only free axis. BT-9b's value ladder then
; dropped the GOAL rung to L* 37 by lowering V (green_goal is HSV(130, 0.86, 0.397)), which is why
; the goal albedos and these edge colours no longer match numerically even though they are the same
; hue. The edge keeps V = 0.850 because an EMISSION colour is not on the value ladder -- the ladder
; governs reflectance under a shared light, and this term is added on top of it.
;
; So: green_goal's hue and saturation (130 deg, 0.86) at the night-edge family's value (0.850).
;   C = 0.850 * 0.86 = 0.731,  m = 0.850 - 0.731 = 0.119,  H' = 130/60 = 2.1667
;   X = C * (1 - |H' mod 2 - 1|) = 0.731 * 0.16667 = 0.1218
;   sector 2 -> (R, G, B) = (m, C + m, X + m) = (0.119, 0.850, 0.241)
; Max/min channel ratio 7.14, identical to the other three by construction -- that ratio is what
; FIX-1 measured on blue (8.90 through the tonemap at night, 1.01 by day) and it is the number
; this material is checkable against.
shader_parameter/edge_color = Color(0.119, 0.850, 0.241, 1)
